#{[17592186480655 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480655 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133261" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480655 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480655 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed as a polynomial in terms of $x,$ the length of Fred's plot of land is{{field-1}}\nmeters." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480655 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4f002498-444e-5f1e-b174-d456ccc0407b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480655 #edb/ref 1074 "The width of the plot is $x+3$ meters, and its area is $2{x}^{2}+6x$ square meters. Since the $\\text{area}=\\text{length}\\times \\text{width},$ we factor the expression for the area to find the length.\n\nFirst, we find the $\\text{GCF}$ of the two terms:\n\n$$\n\\text{GCF}(2{x}^{2},6x)=2x\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite each term in the original expression as a product with the $\\text{GCF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+6x & = \\\\ (2x\\cdot x)+(2x\\cdot 3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we write the $\\text{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x\\cdot x)+(2x\\cdot 3) & = \\\\ 2x(x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we conclude that the length of the plot must be $2x$ meters.\n\n![](images/19/19307236885ccd9bc41d9bdd70df230932f2858804a24e96945880124dee0457.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480664 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480664 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133263" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480664 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480664 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/2e/2ee258ce570d5afc7a014eece4d759c074fbc2dc618fea1eccf26be502ed6e0d.png)\n\nDebbie is Fred's neighbor. She has a plot of land whose area is ${x}^{2}-9$ square meters and whose width is $x+3$ meters, as depicted above. What is the length of Debbie's plot in terms of $x?$\n\nDebbie's plot has a length of\n{{field-1}}\nmeters." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480664 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6574f685-c760-598c-909a-a94bd6f0ab34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480664 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the length of Debbie's plot of land, we need to factor the expression ${x}^{2}-9.$\n\nFirst, we write the expression as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-9={x}^{2}-{3}^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{3}^{2}=(x+3)(x-3)\n$$\n\nAs a result, we conclude that the length of the plot must be $x-3$ meters.\n\n![](images/fe/fec51af6e1d422bb55951e72a328c9b6d7555197303349bbbec9352434aac196.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480700 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480700 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133265" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480700 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480700 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5b/5bbf82f97549272635ff1ea78d945691cf356dfbde2d0933788f734d6b7f9601.png)\n\nFred's neighbor to the south, Emma, has a land plot of area ${x}^{2}+3x-10$ square meters, as shown in the picture. Given that the length of Emma's plot of land is $x+5$ meters, what is the width of the plot in terms of $x?$\n\nEmma's plot width is\n{{field-1}}\nmeters." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480700 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e97c6917-f66f-5f90-bc2c-151969a83727" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480700 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the required width, we need to factor the expression ${x}^{2}+3x-10.$\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of the constant term $(-10).$ The constant term is negative, so that means one of the factors is negative, and the other is positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & \\times (-10) \\\\ (-1) & \\times 10 \\\\ 2 & \\times (-5) \\\\ (-2) & \\times 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+(-10) & =-9 & \\times  \\\\ -1+10 & =9 & \\times  \\\\ 2+(-5) & =-3 & \\times  \\\\ -2+5 & =3 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, write the linear term as $3x=5x-2x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3x-10 & ={x}^{2}+5x-2x-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately, and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x-2x-10 & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}+5x)+(-2x-10) & = \\\\ x(x+5)-2(x+5) & = \\\\ (x-2)(x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we conclude that the width must be $x-2$ meters. \n\n![](images/6b/6bdc252e1952c6e56a971c7d88c126a75a68e3ae48783c7cb65495ed5b4b86b1.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480701 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480701 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133407" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480701 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480701 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/32/327db918054ccc7ac5cb70315023f4714b374994bded724b7451342dfac2d7ee.png)\n\nWhen $x=20\\,\\text{m},$ the dimensions of Emma's plot of land are $25\\,\\text{m}$ and $18\\text{m}.$ A water pipe goes diagonally from one corner to the opposite, as shown above. Find the **exact** length of the pipe.\n\nThe length of the pipe is \n{{field-1}}\nmeters." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480701 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ea9b6670-1349-5317-9261-60b0dc45876e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480701 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that we have a right triangle with legs $a=25\\,\\text{m}$ and $b=18\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n![](images/4a/4ac2862067c8e6e868b634003643ef935dbe652e3b46647466dd6b1dc2cb5172.png)\n\nLet $c$ be the length of the hypotenuse. Then, according to the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{b}^{2} \\\\ c & =\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the values for $a$ and $b$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\sqrt{{25}^{2}+{18}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{625+324} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{949}\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480704 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480704 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133320" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480704 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480704 #edb/ref 1071 "The value $V(t)$ of Fred's plot of land, in thousands of dollars, can be modeled by the function $V(t)=200\\cdot (1.1)^{t},$ where $t$ is the time in years since he bought the land. What is the growth in the value of the plot over the first two years? Round your answer to the nearest thousand dollars.\n\nFred's land grows in value by\n{{field-1}}\n thousand dollars over the first two years." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480704 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f2953556-ba6d-59b1-a416-ed49b0dde4d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480704 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the growth in the value over the first two years, we need to find the value after $2$ years and then subtract the initial cost:\n\n$$\nV(2)-V(0)\n$$\n\nIn the previous part, we were given the initial value of the land:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(0) & =200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find $V(2),$ the value of the land after $2$ years:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(2) & =200\\cdot (1.1)^{(2)} \\\\  & =200\\cdot (1.21) \\\\  & =242\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the growth in the first two years is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(2)-V(0) & =242-200 \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the growth in the value of the land over the first two years was $42$ thousand dollars." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480713 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186480727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480713 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "157c48f1-c0e8-5f8d-935f-059aca9ad0ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480713 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186480636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480715 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186480719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480715 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "226ccdcc-3b60-57f1-a3c3-8f062d46aa86" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480715 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186480651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480717 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186480718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480717 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3db731f4-5240-5811-93c7-3394a14bf49b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480717 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186480655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480718 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186480722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480718 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "43459601-8ced-51fb-b2f5-0490414b2238" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480718 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186480664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480719 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4609de71-75bd-5c7c-bcbb-76be563e0fa0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480719 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186480704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480720 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186480715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480720 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "621177f0-6fe6-52a1-b257-baed0a035831" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480720 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186480701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480721 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186480713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480721 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "763a48e2-4978-537d-8715-a123558c8bc6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480721 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186480700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480722 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186480721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480722 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "82450113-651d-5d2b-bc4c-b0e8045d63dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480722 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186480638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480724 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9fb35eae-9893-5e59-bd3f-76d3e2c4bfec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480724 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186480632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480727 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186480720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480727 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f4ee72dd-2964-5f4e-a1db-59869b8abae4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480727 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186480647 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
